window.CARDS = [{"ch": 1, "q": "Walk through the request flow of a single-server setup.", "a": "<p>(1) The user's domain (e.g. <code>api.mysite.com</code>) goes to DNS, usually a paid third-party service. (2) DNS returns an IP address. (3) The client sends HTTP requests to that IP. (4) The web server returns HTML or JSON.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Why separate the web tier from the data tier?", "a": "<p>So each can be <b>scaled independently</b>. One server running both stops being enough as users grow.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "When would you pick NoSQL over a relational database?", "a": "<ul><li>You need super-low latency</li><li>Data is unstructured or not relational</li><li>You only serialize/deserialize data (JSON, XML, YAML)</li><li>You must store a massive amount of data</li></ul><p>Otherwise, default to relational.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "What are the limits of vertical scaling?", "a": "<p>A hard hardware limit (you can't add unlimited CPU/RAM), no failover or redundancy (one server down = site down, a single point of failure), and high cost for powerful servers.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Why do web servers behind a load balancer use private IPs?", "a": "<p>For security. Clients only reach the load balancer's public IP. Private IPs are reachable only between servers in the same network, not over the internet.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "In master–slave replication, what happens when the master goes down, and why is it tricky?", "a": "<p>A slave is promoted to master and temporarily handles all operations, and a new slave is added. It's tricky because the promoted slave's data may be out of date, so missing data must be recovered with data recovery scripts.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "What happens if a slave database goes offline?", "a": "<p>If it was the only slave, reads go to the master temporarily until a new slave replaces it. If there are other slaves, reads go to the healthy ones and a new server replaces the failed one.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Describe the book's cache read flow. What is the book's name for it?", "a": "<p>Check the cache. On a hit, return the data. On a miss, query the database, store the result in the cache, and return it. The book calls it a <b>read-through cache</b> (often called cache-aside when the app does the loading).</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Name the five cache considerations.", "a": "<p>When to use it (read-heavy, rarely modified, not for persistence), expiration policy (not too short or too long), consistency (store and cache aren't in one transaction), mitigating failures (multiple cache servers across DCs, overprovision memory), and eviction policy (LRU; also LFU, FIFO).</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "How can you remove a file from a CDN before its TTL expires?", "a": "<p>Invalidate it with the CDN vendor's API, or use object versioning: change the URL, e.g. <code>image.png?v=2</code>.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Why are sticky sessions a weak way to scale, and what replaces them?", "a": "<p>They add overhead, make adding/removing servers much harder, and make server failures hard to handle. Instead, make the web tier <b>stateless</b>: keep session data in a shared store (NoSQL in the book), so any server can serve any request and the tier can autoscale.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "What are the three challenges of a multi-data-center setup?", "a": "<p><b>Traffic redirection</b> (geoDNS to the nearest DC), <b>data synchronization</b> (replicate across DCs, e.g. Netflix's async replication), and <b>test and deployment</b> (test from several locations; automated deployment keeps DCs consistent).</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "How does a message queue help with slow photo-processing tasks?", "a": "<p>Web servers publish jobs to the queue and workers process them asynchronously. Each side can be unavailable without blocking the other, and workers scale with queue size: add workers when it grows, remove them when it's mostly empty.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "What makes a good sharding key?", "a": "<p>One or more columns that route each query to the right shard and, above all, <b>spread data evenly</b>, such as <code>user_id</code> with <code>user_id % 4</code>.</p>"}, {"ch": 1, "q": "Name three problems sharding introduces and how to handle each.", "a": "<p><b>Resharding</b> (shard full or exhausted early) → consistent hashing. <b>Celebrity/hotspot key</b> → a dedicated shard per celebrity, possibly split further. <b>Cross-shard joins</b> → de-normalize so queries hit a single table.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "What do 2<sup>10</sup>, 2<sup>20</sup>, 2<sup>30</sup>, 2<sup>40</sup> and 2<sup>50</sup> bytes approximately equal?", "a": "<p>A thousand (1 KB), a million (1 MB), a billion (1 GB), a trillion (1 TB) and a quadrillion (1 PB) bytes.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Put these in order, fastest first: disk seek, L1 cache reference, round trip within a datacenter, main memory reference, California ↔ Netherlands round trip.", "a": "<p>L1 cache (0.5 ns) → main memory (100 ns) → datacenter round trip (500 µs) → disk seek (10 ms) → California ↔ Netherlands (150 ms).</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Roughly how much slower is a disk seek than a main memory reference?", "a": "<p>About 100,000×: 10 ms vs 100 ns.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "What five conclusions does the book draw from the latency numbers?", "a": "<ul><li>Memory is fast but the disk is slow.</li><li>Avoid disk seeks if possible.</li><li>Simple compression algorithms are fast.</li><li>Compress data before sending it over the internet if possible.</li><li>Data centers are in different regions, and sending data between them takes time.</li></ul>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "How much downtime does 99.99% availability allow per year? Per day?", "a": "<p>About 52.6 minutes per year, or 8.64 seconds per day.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "What is an SLA, and where do the big cloud providers set theirs?", "a": "<p>A service level agreement: a formal agreement between a service provider and its customer defining the level of uptime the service will deliver. Amazon, Google and Microsoft set theirs at 99.9% or above.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Walk through the Twitter QPS estimate.", "a": "<p>300 M MAU × 50% = 150 M DAU. 150 M × 2 tweets ÷ 86,400 s ≈ <b>3,500 QPS</b>. Peak = 2 × QPS ≈ <b>7,000</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Walk through the Twitter media storage estimate.", "a": "<p>150 M DAU × 2 tweets × 10% with media × 1 MB = <b>30 TB per day</b>. × 365 × 5 years ≈ <b>55 PB</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Why is it fine to estimate only media storage in the Twitter example?", "a": "<p>IDs and text are tiny by comparison: 300 M tweets × ~204 bytes ≈ 61 GB a day, about 0.2% of the 30 TB of media.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "What's a quick way to convert requests per day into QPS?", "a": "<p>A day has 86,400 s ≈ 10<sup>5</sup>, so divide daily requests by ~100,000. For example, 1 million a day ≈ 12 per second.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "How should you handle \"99,987 / 9.1\" in an interview?", "a": "<p>Round it: 100,000 / 10 = 10,000. Precision isn't expected; the process is what's graded.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Why does the book insist on labeling units?", "a": "<p>A bare \"5\" could mean 5 KB or 5 MB, which can confuse you later. Writing \"5 MB\" removes the ambiguity.</p>"}, {"ch": 2, "q": "Which back-of-the-envelope estimates are most commonly asked?", "a": "<p>QPS, peak QPS, storage, cache and number of servers.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What does a system design interview simulate, and what matters more than the final design?", "a": "<p>Two co-workers collaborating on an ambiguous problem to reach a solution that meets their goals. The design process matters more: showing design skill, defending your choices and responding to feedback constructively.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "Besides technical design skill, what signals is the interviewer looking for?", "a": "<p>Ability to collaborate, to work under pressure, to resolve ambiguity constructively, and to ask good questions.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "Name three red flags. Why is over-engineering one of them?", "a": "<p>Over-engineering, narrow-mindedness and stubbornness. Over-engineering chases design purity, ignores trade-offs and the compounding costs of complex systems, and companies pay a high price for it.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What's the lesson of \"Jimmy\" in Step 1?", "a": "<p>Don't answer quickly without understanding the requirements. Speed earns no points and answering without understanding is a huge red flag. Slow down and ask clarifying questions.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What four questions does the book suggest to start Step 1?", "a": "<ul><li>What specific features are we building?</li><li>How many users does the product have?</li><li>How fast will it scale (in 3 months, 6 months, a year)?</li><li>What's the tech stack, and which existing services can we leverage?</li></ul>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "The interviewer says \"make your own assumptions.\" What do you do?", "a": "<p>State them and write them on the whiteboard or paper, because you'll likely need them later.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What scope did the news feed clarifying dialogue establish?", "a": "<p>Web and mobile; make a post and see friends' feed; reverse chronological order; up to 5,000 friends; 10 million DAU; posts can contain images and videos.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What do you do in Step 2?", "a": "<p>Propose a blueprint and get feedback, treating the interviewer as a teammate; draw a box diagram of key components; run back-of-the-envelope numbers to check the scale (asking first if they're wanted); walk through a few concrete use cases.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "Should the high-level design include API endpoints and a database schema?", "a": "<p>It depends. For a large problem like \"design Google search\" it's too low-level; for the backend of a multiplayer poker game it's fair game. Ask the interviewer.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "What four things should be true before you start the deep dive?", "a": "<p>You've agreed on goals and feature scope, sketched a high-level blueprint, got feedback on it, and have initial ideas from that feedback about which areas to focus on.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "Give two good deep-dive topics and one bad one.", "a": "<p>Good: the hash function in a URL shortener; reducing latency and online/offline status in a chat system. Bad: explaining Facebook's EdgeRank ranking algorithm in detail, which costs time without showing scalable-design skill.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "List the wrap-up talking points.", "a": "<p>Bottlenecks and improvements (never call the design perfect), a recap, error cases, operations (metrics, error logs, rollout), the next scale curve (e.g. 1 M → 10 M users), and refinements with more time.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "How should you split a 45-minute session?", "a": "<p>Step 1: 3–10 min. Step 2: 10–15 min. Step 3: 10–25 min. Step 4: 3–5 min.</p>"}, {"ch": 3, "q": "You're stuck, or you've just finished presenting your design. What should you do?", "a": "<p>If stuck, ask for hints and keep thinking out loud. After presenting, keep going: you're not done until the interviewer says so, so ask for feedback early and often.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Name the three benefits of a rate limiter, with an example for each.", "a": "<p>Prevent resource starvation from DoS (e.g. Twitter's 300 tweets per 3 hours), reduce cost (fewer servers; per-call third-party APIs such as credit checks and payments), and prevent server overload from bots or misbehaving users.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Why not enforce rate limiting on the client?", "a": "<p>Malicious actors can easily forge client requests, and you may not control the client implementation.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "When should the rate limiter go in an API gateway rather than your own server-side code?", "a": "<p>When you already run microservices with an API gateway (for authentication, IP whitelisting, etc.), or lack the engineering resources to build your own. The catch: a third-party gateway may limit your choice of algorithm.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "How does the token bucket algorithm work, and what are its two parameters?", "a": "<p>A refiller adds tokens at a fixed rate up to the bucket's capacity (extras overflow). Each request takes one token; no token means the request is dropped. Parameters: <b>bucket size</b> and <b>refill rate</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "A user may post once per second, add 150 friends per day and like 5 posts per second. How many token buckets per user? What about per-IP limiting, or a 10,000 req/s system-wide cap?", "a": "<p>3 buckets per user (one per endpoint). One bucket per IP address. One global bucket shared by all requests.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "How does the leaking bucket differ from the token bucket, and what's its main drawback?", "a": "<p>Requests go into a FIFO queue (dropped if full) and are processed at a <b>fixed outflow rate</b>. A burst fills the queue with old requests, so recent requests get rate limited if the old ones aren't processed in time.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "What's the fixed window counter's major flaw? Use the book's numbers.", "a": "<p>Bursts at window edges. Limit 5/min: 5 requests between 2:00:30 and 2:01:00 plus 5 between 2:01:00 and 2:01:30 means 10 pass in the one-minute window 2:00:30–2:01:30, twice the limit.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Sliding window log, limit 2/min. Requests arrive at 1:00:01, 1:00:30, 1:00:50 and 1:01:40. Which are accepted?", "a": "<p>Accepted, accepted, <b>rejected</b> (log size 3, but its timestamp stays logged), accepted (1:00:01 and 1:00:30 fall before 1:00:40 and are removed, leaving 2). Accurate in any rolling window, but memory-hungry.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Sliding window counter: limit 7/min, 5 requests last minute, 3 this minute, new request 30% into the minute. Allowed?", "a": "<p>3 + 5 × 0.7 = 6.5, rounded down to 6, which is under 7, so yes. It's an approximation, but Cloudflare found only 0.003% of 400 million requests wrongly allowed or limited.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Why store counters in Redis rather than a database, and which two commands do you use?", "a": "<p>Disk access is too slow; an in-memory cache is fast and supports time-based expiration. <code>INCR</code> increments the counter; <code>EXPIRE</code> sets a timeout after which the counter is deleted.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "What does a throttled client receive, and which headers help clients avoid throttling?", "a": "<p>HTTP <b>429 Too Many Requests</b> with <code>X-Ratelimit-Retry-After</code>. Responses also carry <code>X-Ratelimit-Remaining</code> and <code>X-Ratelimit-Limit</code>.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Describe the race condition in a distributed rate limiter and how to fix it without locks.", "a": "<p>Counter is 3; two requests read 3 concurrently, each writes 4, but it should be 5. Locks work but slow the system significantly; use a <b>Lua script</b> or Redis <b>sorted sets</b> instead.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "You run several rate limiter servers. Why not use sticky sessions, and what's better?", "a": "<p>Sticky sessions are neither scalable nor flexible. Use a <b>centralized data store such as Redis</b> that every limiter reads and writes.</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "Monitoring shows the limiter breaks down during flash sales. What do you change?", "a": "<p>Switch to an algorithm that supports burst traffic, such as the <b>token bucket</b>. (If valid requests are being dropped in general, relax the rules instead.)</p>"}, {"ch": 4, "q": "What's the difference between hard and soft rate limiting, and at which other OSI layer can you limit?", "a": "<p>Hard: requests can never exceed the threshold. Soft: they can exceed it for a short period. Besides layer 7 (HTTP), you can limit by IP address at layer 3, e.g. with iptables.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "Why does <code>serverIndex = hash(key) % N</code> break when a server is added or removed?", "a": "<p>N changes, so <code>hash % N</code> gives a different index for most keys, not just those on the changed server. Clients go to the wrong servers and you get a storm of cache misses.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "In the book's example (8 keys, 4 servers, server 1 goes offline), how many keys move, and how many had to?", "a": "<p>5 of 8 move (key0, key3, key4, key5, key7). Only key0 and key4 were on server 1; the other three moved needlessly.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "By the definition, how many keys are remapped on average when a consistent-hashing table is resized?", "a": "<p><b>k/n</b>, where k is the number of keys and n the number of slots. A traditional hash table remaps nearly all keys.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "What is the hash space if you use SHA-1, and how do you get the ring?", "a": "<p>0 to 2<sup>160</sup> − 1. Join the two ends of that range so the last value wraps around to the first.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "How are servers and keys placed on the ring, and how do you find a key's server?", "a": "<p>Hash servers (by IP or name) and keys with the same function, with no modulo. From the key's position, go clockwise to the first server.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "Server 4 is added between key0 and server 0. Which keys move?", "a": "<p>Only key0, which moves from server 0 to server 4. In general, only keys between the new node and the previous server anticlockwise move, and all of them go to the new node.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "Server 1 is removed. Where do its keys go?", "a": "<p>To the next server clockwise (server 2). The affected range runs from s1 anticlockwise to s0. Nothing else moves.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "What are the two problems with the basic (one point per server) ring?", "a": "<ul><li>Partitions can't stay equal as servers come and go (removing s1 makes s2's partition twice as large as s0's and s3's).</li><li>Keys can be unevenly distributed (most keys on server 2, none on servers 1 and 3).</li></ul>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "What is a virtual node, and how does lookup work with them?", "a": "<p>One of many points on the ring that stand for a real server (s0_0, s0_1, s0_2 for server 0). Go clockwise to the first virtual node and map it to its server.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "What standard deviation do 100 and 200 virtual nodes give, and what is the cost?", "a": "<p>About 10% of the mean with 100 and about 5% with 200. More virtual nodes need more space to store their metadata, so tune the number to the system's needs.</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "Name three benefits of consistent hashing.", "a": "<p>Few keys move when servers change, horizontal scaling is easy because data is spread evenly, and it mitigates hotspot shards (several celebrities' data on one shard).</p>"}, {"ch": 5, "q": "Name real systems that use consistent hashing.", "a": "<p>Amazon Dynamo (partitioning), Apache Cassandra (data partitioning), Discord, the Akamai CDN, and Google's Maglev load balancer.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "What are the seven requirements for this key-value store?", "a": "<p>Pairs smaller than 10 KB, ability to store big data, high availability, high scalability, automatic scaling, tunable consistency, and low latency.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Define consistency, availability and partition tolerance. Why can't a CA system exist in practice?", "a": "<p>C: all clients see the same data at the same time. A: every request gets a response even if some nodes are down. P: the system keeps working despite communication breaks between nodes. Network failure is unavoidable, so a distributed system must tolerate partitions, which rules out CA.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Three replicas; n3 is cut off from n1 and n2. What does a CP system do, and what does an AP system do?", "a": "<p><b>CP:</b> blocks writes to n1 and n2 so nothing diverges, making the system unavailable (a bank returns an error). <b>AP:</b> keeps accepting reads (possibly stale) and writes on n1 and n2, then syncs n3 when the partition heals.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Which technique partitions the data, and what two advantages does it bring here?", "a": "<p>Consistent hashing: it spreads keys evenly and minimizes movement when nodes change. It enables <b>automatic scaling</b> and <b>heterogeneity</b> (virtual nodes proportional to server capacity).</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "How are a key's N replicas chosen, and what two refinements improve reliability?", "a": "<p>Walk clockwise from the key's ring position and take the first N servers. Count only <b>unique physical servers</b> (virtual nodes can repeat a server), and place replicas in <b>distinct data centers</b>, since nodes in one data center often fail together.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "With N = 3, what does W = 1 actually mean?", "a": "<p>The data is still sent to all three replicas. The coordinator just considers the write successful after the first acknowledgment, without waiting for the other two.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "When is strong consistency guaranteed? Give the fast-read and fast-write configurations.", "a": "<p>When <b>W + R &gt; N</b>, because read and write quorums must overlap in at least one node holding the latest data (typically N = 3, W = R = 2). Fast reads: R = 1, W = N. Fast writes: W = 1, R = N. W + R ≤ N gives no guarantee.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Strong vs weak vs eventual consistency: which does this design choose, and why?", "a": "<p>Strong: reads always see the latest write. Weak: reads may miss it. Eventual: replicas converge given enough time. The design uses <b>eventual</b> consistency (like Dynamo and Cassandra), because strong consistency blocks operations until all replicas agree, which hurts availability.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "In the vector clock example, why is D5 <code>([Sx, 3], [Sy, 1], [Sz, 1])</code>?", "a": "<p>D3 <code>([Sx, 2], [Sy, 1])</code> and D4 <code>([Sx, 2], [Sz, 1])</code> conflict. A client reconciles them, and Sx handles the write, so Sx's counter goes from 2 to 3 while Sy and Sz carry over.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "How do you tell from two vector clocks whether they conflict?", "a": "<p>X is an ancestor of Y if every counter in Y is ≥ X's. They conflict (siblings) if each has some counter smaller than the other's, e.g. <code>([s0, 1], [s1, 2])</code> vs <code>([s0, 2], [s1, 1])</code>.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "What are the two downsides of vector clocks, and how is clock growth handled?", "a": "<p>Clients must implement conflict resolution, and the pairs can grow fast. Cap the length and drop the oldest pairs. This can make reconciliation inaccurate, but Amazon hasn't seen the problem in production.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Why gossip instead of all-to-all multicast, and how does gossip mark a node down?", "a": "<p>All-to-all is inefficient with many servers. With gossip, each node keeps a membership list of heartbeat counters, increments its own, and sends heartbeats to random nodes. If a member's counter doesn't increase for a set period, it is considered offline, and it's marked down once other nodes confirm.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "What are sloppy quorum and hinted handoff?", "a": "<p><b>Sloppy quorum:</b> use the first W (or R) <i>healthy</i> servers on the ring and ignore offline ones. <b>Hinted handoff:</b> a stand-in server (s3 for a down s2) handles requests temporarily and pushes the data back when the failed server recovers.</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "How do Merkle trees repair permanent failures efficiently?", "a": "<p>Hash keys, then buckets, then up to a root. Compare roots and descend only into subtrees whose hashes differ, then sync just those buckets. Transfer is proportional to the differences, not the data size (e.g. 1 million buckets for 1 billion keys = 1,000 keys per bucket).</p>"}, {"ch": 6, "q": "Describe the write path and the read path on a node.", "a": "<p><b>Write:</b> append to the commit log, save in the memory cache, flush to an SSTable when the cache hits its threshold. <b>Read:</b> check the memory cache; on a miss, use the bloom filter to find which SSTables might hold the key, read them, return the result.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Why doesn't a database <code>auto_increment</code> primary key work for a distributed ID generator?", "a": "<p>A single database server isn't large enough for the load, and generating unique IDs across multiple databases with low delay is hard.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "What are the five requirements in this chapter's problem?", "a": "<p>Unique, numeric only, fits in 64 bits, ordered by date, and more than 10,000 IDs per second.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "How does multi-master replication generate IDs, and what are its three drawbacks?", "a": "<p>Each database's <code>auto_increment</code> steps by <i>k</i> (the number of servers). Drawbacks: hard to scale across data centers, IDs don't increase with time across servers, and adding or removing a server breaks the scheme.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Why is UUID rejected here even though it needs no coordination?", "a": "<p>It's 128 bits (we need 64), it isn't sortable by time, and it can be non-numeric.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "What is a ticket server and what is its main weakness?", "a": "<p>A single central database that hands out <code>auto_increment</code> IDs (Flickr's approach). It is a single point of failure, and running several of them brings back synchronization problems.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Draw the Snowflake bit layout.", "a": "<p><code>1 sign | 41 timestamp | 5 datacenter | 5 machine | 12 sequence</code>, totaling 64 bits.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Why are Snowflake IDs sortable by time?", "a": "<p>The timestamp occupies the most significant bits, so a later millisecond always gives a larger number.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "How long does a 41-bit millisecond timestamp last, and how do you push overflow further away?", "a": "<p>2<sup>41</sup> − 1 ms ≈ 69 years. Use a custom epoch close to today, since the years count from the epoch.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "How many IDs can one machine generate per millisecond, and why?", "a": "<p>4,096, because the sequence field has 12 bits (2<sup>12</sup>). It resets to 0 each millisecond.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Which Snowflake fields are fixed at startup and which are generated at runtime? What's the risk with the fixed ones?", "a": "<p>Datacenter and machine IDs are fixed at startup. Timestamp and sequence are generated at runtime. Accidentally changing or duplicating datacenter/machine IDs can cause ID collisions.</p>"}, {"ch": 7, "q": "Name three wrap-up talking points for this design.", "a": "<p>Clock synchronization (NTP), tuning section lengths (e.g. more timestamp bits for low-concurrency, long-lived apps), and high availability.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "What are the traffic numbers for this design, and how do you derive them?", "a": "<p>100 million writes/day ÷ 24 ÷ 3600 ≈ <b>1,160 writes/s</b>. With a 10:1 read:write ratio, <b>11,600 reads/s</b>. Over 10 years, 100 million × 365 × 10 = <b>365 billion</b> records.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "How much storage do 10 years of URLs need at 100 bytes each?", "a": "<p>365 billion × 100 bytes ≈ <b>36.5 TB</b>. The book prints 365 TB because it multiplies by 10 years a second time.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "What are the two API endpoints?", "a": "<p><code>POST api/v1/data/shorten</code> with <code>{longUrl: longURLString}</code>, which returns the shortURL. <code>GET api/v1/shortUrl</code>, which returns the longURL for the HTTP redirect.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "301 or 302: which one reduces server load, which one is better for analytics, and why?", "a": "<p><b>301</b> (permanent) is cached by the browser, so only the first request reaches the shortener, which means less load. <b>302</b> (temporary) sends every request through the shortener first, so you can track click rate and source.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Why store the mapping in a relational database rather than a hash table?", "a": "<p>A hash table in memory isn't feasible for real systems: memory is limited and expensive. The table is <code>url(id PK auto increment, shortURL, longURL)</code>.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Why is the hashValue 7 characters long?", "a": "<p>It uses 62 characters (10 + 26 + 26). You need the smallest n with 62<sup>n</sup> ≥ 365 billion. 62<sup>6</sup> ≈ 56.8 billion is too few; 62<sup>7</sup> ≈ 3.5 trillion is enough.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Why not just use CRC32, MD5 or SHA-1 output as the short code?", "a": "<p>The outputs are too long (CRC32 is 8 hex characters, MD5 32, SHA-1 40). Keeping only the first 7 characters causes collisions, which must then be resolved.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "How does hash + collision resolution handle a collision, and what makes it slow?", "a": "<p>It appends a predefined string to the long URL and hashes again, repeating until the code doesn't exist in the DB. Checking the DB on every request is expensive; a bloom filter speeds up the membership test.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Convert 11157 to base 62.", "a": "<p>11157 = 2 × 62² + 55 × 62 + 59 → [2, 55, 59] → <b>2TX</b>, since 55 = T and 59 = X.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Compare hash + collision resolution with base 62 conversion.", "a": "<ul><li>Length: fixed vs grows with the ID.</li><li>Unique ID generator: not needed vs required.</li><li>Collisions: possible vs impossible.</li><li>Next URL guessable: no vs yes if IDs increment by 1 (a security concern).</li></ul>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Walk through the URL shortening flow.", "a": "<p>If the longURL is already in the DB, return its shortURL. Otherwise get a new ID from the unique ID generator, convert it to base 62, and save (id, shortURL, longURL).</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Walk through the redirect flow. What happens if the short URL isn't found?", "a": "<p>Load balancer → web server → cache; on a miss, read the database; then return the longURL. If it's not in the database either, the user probably entered an invalid shortURL.</p>"}, {"ch": 8, "q": "Name the wrap-up talking points.", "a": "<p>Rate limiter (against floods of shortening requests), web server scaling (stateless tier), database replication and sharding, analytics, and availability/consistency/reliability.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "Name the four main uses of a web crawler, with an example of each.", "a": "<p>Search engine indexing (Googlebot, the most common), web archiving (national libraries such as the US Library of Congress), web mining (financial firms pulling annual reports), and web monitoring (Digimarc finding pirated works).</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What scope did the interviewer set for this crawler?", "a": "<p>Search engine indexing, 1 billion pages per month, HTML only, include newly added or edited pages, store HTML pages for up to 5 years, ignore duplicate content.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What are the four characteristics of a good crawler?", "a": "<p><b>Scalability</b> (parallelize across billions of pages), <b>robustness</b> (survive bad HTML, unresponsive servers, malicious links), <b>politeness</b> (don't hammer one site), <b>extensibility</b> (add content types with minimal change).</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "Walk through the capacity estimate.", "a": "<p>1B pages ÷ 30 days ÷ 24 h ÷ 3,600 s ≈ <b>400 QPS</b>, peak <b>800</b>. At 500 KB per page that's <b>500 TB/month</b>, and 500 TB × 12 × 5 = <b>30 PB</b> over five years.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "How does “Content Seen?” detect duplicate pages, and why not compare the HTML directly?", "a": "<p>It compares <b>hash values</b> of pages. Character-by-character comparison is too slow across billions of pages, and about 29% of pages are duplicates, so the check is worth doing.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What does “URL Seen?” track, why is it needed, and how is it implemented?", "a": "<p>URLs already visited or already in the frontier. Without it the same URL gets added repeatedly, which raises server load and can cause infinite loops. It is typically a <b>Bloom filter</b> or a <b>hash table</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "Why do crawlers use BFS instead of DFS, and what two problems does plain BFS have?", "a": "<p>DFS can go very deep. BFS with a FIFO queue is <b>impolite</b>, because most links point to the same host, so parallel downloads flood it. It also <b>ignores priority</b>, because every URL is treated as equally important.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "How does the URL frontier enforce politeness?", "a": "<p>A queue router uses a mapping table (host → queue) so each FIFO queue b1…bn holds one host's URLs. The queue selector maps each worker thread to one queue, and the worker downloads one page at a time, optionally with a delay between downloads.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What do the front queues and back queues of the URL frontier each do?", "a": "<p><b>Front queues</b> manage priority: a prioritizer scores URLs by usefulness (PageRank, traffic, update frequency) into queues f1…fn, and a selector picks randomly with a bias toward high priority. <b>Back queues</b> manage politeness, one host per queue.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "How do you keep crawled data fresh without recrawling everything?", "a": "<p>Recrawl based on each page's update history, and prioritize so important pages are recrawled first and more often.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "Where does the URL frontier live, given hundreds of millions of URLs?", "a": "<p>Hybrid: most URLs on disk, with in-memory buffers for enqueue/dequeue that are periodically flushed to disk. Memory alone isn't durable or scalable, and disk alone is a slow bottleneck.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What is robots.txt and how should the crawler handle it?", "a": "<p>The Robots Exclusion Protocol file, where a site lists which pages crawlers may download. Check it before crawling a site, follow its rules, and cache it, refreshing the cache periodically.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "Name four HTML downloader performance optimizations. Why does the DNS one matter?", "a": "<p>Distributed crawl, DNS cache, locality, short timeout. DNS lookups take 10–200 ms and many DNS interfaces are synchronous, so one lookup can block other threads. A cron-refreshed DNS cache avoids that.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "What is a spider trap and how do you defend against it?", "a": "<p>A page that loops the crawler forever, e.g. <code>/foo/bar/foo/bar/…</code>. Cap URL length. Spot trap sites by their unusually large page counts, then exclude them or add custom URL filters after manual verification.</p>"}, {"ch": 9, "q": "How would you crawl links that JavaScript generates on the fly?", "a": "<p>Perform server-side rendering (dynamic rendering) before parsing, so dynamically generated links appear in the page the parser sees.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What are the requirements and daily volumes for this notification system?", "a": "<p>Push, SMS and email; soft real-time; iOS, Android and laptop/desktop; triggered by clients or scheduled server-side; users can opt out. Daily: <b>10M push, 1M SMS, 5M email</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What does \"soft real-time\" mean here?", "a": "<p>Deliver as soon as possible, but a slight delay is acceptable when the system is under high workload.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "Which third-party service delivers each channel?", "a": "<p>iOS → APNs, Android → FCM, SMS → Twilio or Nexmo, email → Sendgrid or Mailchimp.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What two things does a provider send to APNs?", "a": "<p>A <b>device token</b> (unique identifier of the target device) and a <b>payload</b> (a JSON dictionary with the notification content, e.g. <code>aps.alert.title</code>, <code>body</code>, <code>badge</code>).</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "When and where is contact info collected, and why are device tokens in their own table?", "a": "<p>API servers collect it when a user installs the app or signs up, and store it in the DB. Email and phone go in <code>user</code>; tokens go in <code>device</code> because one user can have many devices, and a push can go to all of them.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "Why must third-party integration be extensible?", "a": "<p>A provider may be unavailable in new markets or in the future. For example, FCM is unavailable in China, where alternatives like Jpush or PushY are used. You need to plug providers in and out easily.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What are the three problems with the single-notification-server design?", "a": "<p>Single point of failure; hard to scale DB, cache and processing components independently; performance bottleneck from resource-intensive work like building HTML and waiting on third parties, especially at peak.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What three changes produce the improved design?", "a": "<p>Move the DB and cache out of the notification server, add more notification servers with automatic horizontal scaling, and introduce message queues to decouple components.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "Why give each notification type its own message queue?", "a": "<p>So an outage at one third-party service doesn't affect other notification types. Queues also buffer high volumes.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "List the six steps of sending a notification in the improved design.", "a": "<p>1) Service calls notification server API. 2) Server fetches user info, device token and settings from cache/DB. 3) Event goes to its channel's queue. 4) Workers pull events. 5) Workers send to the third party. 6) Third party delivers to the device.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "How does the system avoid losing notifications?", "a": "<p>It persists notification data in a notification log database and retries failures by putting them back on the queue (a predefined number of times), alerting developers if the problem persists.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "Can you guarantee exactly-once delivery? What do you do instead?", "a": "<p>No; distributed failures can cause duplicates. Dedupe by event ID: if an arriving event's ID was seen before, discard it; otherwise send.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What is the notification setting table, and when is it checked?", "a": "<p><code>user_id</code> (bigInt), <code>channel</code> (push, email or SMS), <code>opt_in</code> (boolean). It's checked before any notification is sent.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "How are push notification APIs secured?", "a": "<p>With an appKey and appSecret, so only authenticated or verified clients can send push notifications.</p>"}, {"ch": 10, "q": "What is the key metric to monitor, and how do you react when it grows?", "a": "<p>The total number of queued notifications. A large number means workers are too slow, so add more workers.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "What are the requirements for this news feed system?", "a": "<p>Web and mobile; publish posts and see friends' posts; reverse chronological order; up to 5,000 friends; 10M DAU; posts can contain images and videos.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "What are the two core APIs and their params?", "a": "<p><code>POST /v1/me/feed</code> with <code>content</code> and <code>auth_token</code> to publish; <code>GET /v1/me/feed</code> with <code>auth_token</code> to retrieve.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Which three services do the web servers call when a user publishes a post?", "a": "<p>The post service (persists the post to DB and cache), the fanout service (pushes it to friends' news feed caches), and the notification service (tells friends and sends push notifications).</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "What two things do web servers enforce on publishing, and why?", "a": "<p>Authentication (only users with a valid <code>auth_token</code> can post) and rate limiting (caps posts per user per period, to prevent spam and abusive content).</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Give the pros and cons of fanout on write.", "a": "<p>Pros: real-time, and fetching is fast because feeds are pre-computed. Cons: slow for users with many friends (the hotkey problem), and wasted work for inactive users.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Give the pros and cons of fanout on read.", "a": "<p>Pros: no wasted work for inactive users, and no hotkey problem. Con: fetching the feed is slow because it isn't pre-computed.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Which fanout model does the design use, and how does it handle celebrities?", "a": "<p>A hybrid: push for most users so feeds load fast; for celebrities or users with many followers, followers pull their posts on demand. Consistent hashing helps spread hotkeys evenly.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Walk through the fanout service's five steps.", "a": "<p>1) Get friend IDs from the graph DB. 2) Get friend info from the user cache and filter by settings (muted, selective sharing). 3) Send the friend list and post ID to the message queue. 4) Fanout workers consume from the queue. 5) Store <code>&lt;post_id, user_id&gt;</code> in the news feed cache.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Why does the news feed cache store only IDs, and why is a size limit OK?", "a": "<p>Full user and post objects would use too much memory. A configurable limit is fine because most users only read the latest content, so the cache miss rate stays low.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Why can a friend's post be missing from your feed even though you're friends?", "a": "<p>The fanout service filters by settings: you may have muted them, or they may have shared the post only with specific friends or hidden it from you.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "What does \"hydrating\" the feed mean on retrieval?", "a": "<p>The news feed service turns the list of post IDs from the feed cache into full objects (username, profile picture, content, images) by fetching from the user cache and post cache, then returns JSON.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Where are images and videos served from?", "a": "<p>A CDN, for fast retrieval.</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Name the five cache layers.", "a": "<p>News feed (feed IDs), content (post data, with a hot cache for popular posts), social graph (follower/following), action (liked, replied, others), counters (likes, replies, followers, following).</p>"}, {"ch": 11, "q": "Which metrics does the book suggest monitoring?", "a": "<p>QPS during peak hours and latency while users refresh their news feed.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "What are the agreed requirements and scale for this chat system?", "a": "<p>1-on-1 chat with low latency, small group chat (max 100 members), online presence, multiple devices per account, push notifications; mobile and web; text only, under 100,000 characters; 50 million DAU; history stored forever.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Why is HTTP fine for sending messages but not for receiving them?", "a": "<p>Sending is client-initiated, and keep-alive keeps the connection open so handshakes are avoided. Receiving needs the server to push, which HTTP's client-initiated model can't do directly.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "What are long polling's three drawbacks?", "a": "<p>With stateless, round-robin servers, the server that gets a message may not hold the recipient's long-poll connection; the server can't tell if a client disconnected; and idle users still reconnect after every timeout.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "How does a WebSocket connection start, why does it pass firewalls, and why use it for sending too?", "a": "<p>The client opens an HTTP connection and upgrades it via a handshake into a persistent, bidirectional connection. It uses ports 80/443 like HTTP/HTTPS. Since it's bidirectional, one protocol for both directions simplifies client and server.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Which service is stateful, and why?", "a": "<p>Only the chat service: each client keeps a persistent connection to one chat server and normally stays on it while that server is available. Login, signup, profile and service discovery are stateless HTTP services.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Could one server hold all connections? Why not do it?", "a": "<p>In theory yes: 1M concurrent users × ~10 KB per connection ≈ 10 GB of memory. But a single server is a deal breaker, mainly because it's a single point of failure. It's only acceptable as a starting point.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Why store chat history in a key-value store? Give the four reasons.", "a": "<p>Easy horizontal scaling; very low latency; relational DBs handle the long tail poorly (large indexes make random access expensive); and it's proven: Facebook Messenger uses HBase and Discord uses Cassandra.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Why order messages by <code>message_id</code> instead of <code>created_at</code>? What's the group table's key?", "a": "<p>Two messages can share a <code>created_at</code>. The group table uses composite primary key <code>(channel_id, message_id)</code>, with <code>channel_id</code> as partition key because all group queries run within a channel.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "What are the options for generating <code>message_id</code>, and why does a local generator suffice?", "a": "<p><code>auto_increment</code> (usually missing in NoSQL), a global 64-bit generator like Snowflake, or a local per-channel sequence. Local works because order only matters within one 1-on-1 or group channel, and it's easier to build.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Walk through the 1-on-1 message flow.", "a": "<p>A → chat server 1 → ID from ID generator → message sync queue → stored in KV store → if B is online, forwarded to chat server 2 and then to B over WebSocket; if offline, the PN servers send a push notification.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "How do a user's multiple devices stay in sync?", "a": "<p>Each device keeps its own <code>cur_max_message_id</code> and fetches from the KV store every message whose recipient is the logged-in user and whose ID is greater than that value.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "How does small group chat deliver messages, and why only for small groups?", "a": "<p>The sender's message is copied into each member's message sync queue (inbox), so clients check only their own inbox. A copy per member is cheap for small groups (WeChat caps at 500) but unacceptable for very large ones.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "Why use heartbeats instead of marking users offline on disconnect? Give the book's numbers.", "a": "<p>Users drop and reconnect often (e.g. in a tunnel), so the naive approach makes the indicator flicker. With heartbeats every 5 s, a user is marked offline only after no heartbeat for x = 30 s.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "How do friends learn about a status change, and what breaks for large groups?", "a": "<p>Pub-sub with one channel per friend pair (A-B, A-C, A-D). In a 100,000-member group each change creates 100,000 events, so fetch status only when a user enters the group or refreshes the friend list.</p>"}, {"ch": 12, "q": "What happens when a chat server goes down?", "a": "<p>Its clients (possibly hundreds of thousands of connections) lose their WebSockets; service discovery (Zookeeper) gives them a new chat server to reconnect to. Unsent messages are handled with retry and queueing.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What are the five requirements, and what is the latency target?", "a": "<p>Fast response (within <b>100 ms</b>, per Facebook's typeahead article, or it stutters), relevant, sorted by popularity, scalable, and highly available.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Walk through the QPS and storage estimates.", "a": "<p>10M DAU × 10 searches × 20 requests (one per character) ÷ 86,400 s ≈ <b>~24,000 QPS</b>; peak ×2 ≈ <b>~48,000</b>. Storage: 10M × 10 × 20 bytes × 20% new = <b>0.4 GB/day</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Why are there about 20 requests per search query?", "a": "<p>The client requests suggestions on every character typed, and an average query is 4 words × 5 characters = 20 characters.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What does the high-level query service run, and why doesn't it scale?", "a": "<p><code>SELECT * FROM frequency_table WHERE query LIKE 'prefix%' ORDER BY frequency DESC LIMIT 5</code>. On a large data set, database access becomes the bottleneck.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What are the steps and time complexity of the basic trie top-k algorithm?", "a": "<p>Find the prefix node O(p), traverse its subtree for valid children O(c), sort and take top k O(c log c). Total O(p) + O(c) + O(c log c); the worst case traverses the whole trie.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Which two optimizations make lookup O(1), and what do they cost?", "a": "<p>Cap the prefix length (e.g. 50), making find O(1), and cache the top k (5) queries at every node, making retrieval O(1). The cost is a lot of extra space, which is worth it for fast responses.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Why not update the trie in real time on every query?", "a": "<p>Billions of queries a day would significantly slow down the query service, and top suggestions don't change much once the trie is built.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Name the components of the data gathering pipeline, in order.", "a": "<p>Analytics logs → aggregators → aggregated data → workers → Trie DB → Trie Cache (weekly snapshot of the DB).</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What are the two options for storing the trie in the Trie DB?", "a": "<p>A document store (e.g. MongoDB) holding a serialized weekly snapshot, or a key-value store where every prefix is a key and that node's data is the value.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Describe the query service flow, including a cache miss.", "a": "<p>Load balancer → API servers → Trie Cache, which builds the suggestions. On a miss (cache server out of memory or offline), data is replenished from the Trie DB so later requests for that prefix hit the cache.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What do <code>cache-control: private, max-age=3600</code> mean for autocomplete results?", "a": "<p><code>private</code>: for a single user only, not to be stored in a shared cache. <code>max-age=3600</code>: valid for 3,600 seconds (1 hour) in the browser cache.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Why is updating a single trie node expensive, and what's the preferred update method?", "a": "<p>Every ancestor up to the root caches its descendants' top queries, so all of them must be updated too. Prefer rebuilding the trie weekly and replacing the old one.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "How do you remove harmful suggestions?", "a": "<p>A filter layer in front of the Trie Cache removes them using configurable rules, and they are physically deleted from the database asynchronously so the next build excludes them.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "What's wrong with sharding by first character, and how do you fix it?", "a": "<p>Distribution is uneven (far more words start with 'c' than 'x'). Use historical data to balance shards (e.g. 's' alone vs 'u'–'z' together) and a shard map manager to look up which shard holds a prefix.</p>"}, {"ch": 13, "q": "Why does the design fail for trending queries, and what helps?", "a": "<p>Workers rebuild only weekly and building takes too long. Ideas: shard to shrink the working set, weight recent queries more in ranking, and use stream processing (Spark Streaming, Storm, Kafka).</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "What scope and constraints come out of the clarifying questions?", "a": "<p>Upload and watch videos; mobile apps, web and smart TV; 5M DAU spending 30 min/day; many international users; most resolutions and formats accepted; encryption required; max video size 1 GB; existing cloud services may be used.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Estimate daily storage and daily CDN cost. Show the arithmetic.", "a": "<p>Storage: 5M × 10% uploaders × 300 MB = <b>150 TB/day</b>. CDN: 5M users × 5 videos × 0.3 GB × $0.02/GB (CloudFront, all US traffic, streaming only) = <b>$150,000/day</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Which cloud services do you rent, why not build them, and what does each high-level component handle?", "a": "<p>Rent a CDN and blob storage: they're extremely complex and costly to build, and interviews reward choosing the right tool (Netflix uses AWS, Facebook uses Akamai). The CDN serves video streams; API servers handle everything else (recommendations, upload URLs, metadata, signup).</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Walk through uploading the actual video, step by step.", "a": "<p>1) Upload to original storage. 2) Transcoding servers fetch and transcode it. 3a) Transcoded video goes to transcoded storage, then the CDN. 3b) A completion event goes to the completion queue; completion handler workers pull it and update the metadata DB and cache. 4) API servers tell the client it's ready to stream. In parallel, the client sends the metadata (file name, size, format) to API servers, which update the metadata cache and DB.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "How is streaming different from downloading, and where do videos stream from?", "a": "<p>Downloading copies the whole file first; streaming loads a little data at a time so playback starts immediately. Videos stream from the closest CDN edge server using a streaming protocol such as MPEG-DASH, Apple HLS, Microsoft Smooth Streaming or Adobe HDS.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Give four reasons to transcode videos.", "a": "<p>Raw video is huge (an hour of HD at 60 fps can be hundreds of GB); devices and browsers support only certain formats; higher resolution should go to high-bandwidth users and lower to low-bandwidth users; and quality must switch as network conditions change.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "What's the difference between a container and a codec?", "a": "<p>A container (<code>.avi</code>, <code>.mov</code>, <code>.mp4</code>) is the \"basket\" holding the video, audio and metadata. A codec (H.264, VP9, HEVC) is the compression/decompression algorithm that shrinks the video while preserving quality.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Why model transcoding as a DAG, and what tasks might it contain?", "a": "<p>Creators need different pipelines and transcoding is expensive, so client programmers define tasks in stages that run sequentially or in parallel (as in Facebook's video engine). Tasks: inspection, video encodings (360p to 4k), thumbnail, watermark, audio encoding, then assembly.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Name the six components of the transcoding architecture and the preprocessor's four jobs.", "a": "<p>Preprocessor, DAG scheduler, resource manager, task workers, temporary storage, encoded video. The preprocessor splits video into GOPs, splits on behalf of old clients that can't, generates the DAG from config files, and caches GOPs and metadata in temporary storage for retries.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "How does the resource manager schedule work?", "a": "<p>The task scheduler takes the highest-priority task from the task queue, picks the optimal worker from the worker queue, tells that worker to run it, records the task/worker pair in the running queue, and removes it from the running queue when done.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "What are the three speed optimizations?", "a": "<p>Split uploads into GOP-aligned chunks on the client and upload them in parallel (also resumable); use CDN-based upload centers close to users; put message queues between modules so each processes events in parallel instead of waiting for the previous step.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "How do you keep uploads and videos safe?", "a": "<p>Uploads: the client calls <code>POST /upload</code> to get a pre-signed URL (S3 term; Azure calls it a Shared Access Signature) and uploads with it. Videos: DRM (Apple FairPlay, Google Widevine, Microsoft PlayReady), AES encryption with an authorization policy, or visual watermarking.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "The CDN costs $150k/day. How do you cut it?", "a": "<p>Exploit the long tail: serve only popular videos from the CDN and the rest from your own high-capacity video servers; keep fewer encoded versions of unpopular content and encode short videos on demand; distribute regionally popular videos only to their region; at large scale, build your own CDN and partner with ISPs.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "Recoverable vs non-recoverable errors: how is each handled, and what if the metadata DB master dies?", "a": "<p>Recoverable (a segment fails to transcode): retry a few times, then return an error code if it still fails. Non-recoverable (malformed format): stop the video's running tasks and return an error code. If the DB master dies, promote a replica (slave) to master.</p>"}, {"ch": 14, "q": "How does live streaming differ from this design?", "a": "<p>Both need uploading, encoding and streaming, but live streaming has a stricter latency requirement (maybe a different streaming protocol), a lower need for parallelism (small chunks are already processed in real time), and needs error handling that doesn't take long.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "What are the functional and non-functional requirements?", "a": "<p>Functional: add (upload) and download files, sync across devices, see revisions, share files, and notify on edit, delete or share. Non-functional: reliability (no data loss), fast sync, low bandwidth use, scalability, high availability. Collaborative Google Docs editing is out of scope.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Walk through the back-of-the-envelope estimation.", "a": "<p>50M users × 10 GB = <b>500 PB</b> allocated. 10M DAU × 2 uploads / 86,400 s ≈ <b>240 upload QPS</b> (≈ 231 exactly). Peak = 2 × QPS = <b>480</b>.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "What are the three APIs, and when do you use simple vs resumable upload?", "a": "<p><code>/files/upload</code> (<code>uploadType</code>, <code>data</code>), <code>/files/download</code> (<code>path</code>) and <code>/files/list_revisions</code> (<code>path</code>, <code>limit</code>), all authenticated over HTTPS. Simple upload is for small files. Resumable upload is for large files on unreliable networks: get a resumable URL, upload while monitoring state, and resume if disturbed.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Your single server is nearly full. What do you change, in order?", "a": "<p>Shard file storage by <code>user_id</code> across servers. Then, to avoid data loss, move files to Amazon S3 with same-region and cross-region replication. Finally, add a load balancer and more web servers, and move the metadata DB out (with replication and sharding).</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "How are sync conflicts resolved, and what does the losing user see?", "a": "<p>The first version processed wins; the later one gets a conflict. The system shows that user both copies (their local \"conflicted copy\" and the latest server version), and they can merge them or override one with the other.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "What does a block server do with a new file, and what is the block size?", "a": "<p>It splits the file into blocks of up to <b>4 MB</b> (Dropbox's size), compresses each block, encrypts each block, and uploads the blocks to cloud storage.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Which two techniques cut bandwidth on updates?", "a": "<p><b>Delta sync</b>: only modified blocks are sent, not the whole file. <b>Compression</b>: blocks are compressed with an algorithm that suits the file type (e.g. gzip or bzip2 for text).</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "How do you get strong consistency for metadata, and why a relational DB?", "a": "<p>Keep cache replicas consistent with the master, and invalidate caches on every DB write. Use a relational DB because it supports ACID natively; NoSQL would need ACID built into the sync logic.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Name the metadata tables. How do you rebuild a specific version of a file?", "a": "<p>user, device, namespace (workspace), file, file_version and block. Join all the blocks of that <code>file_version</code> in <code>block_order</code>. Existing <code>file_version</code> rows are read-only.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Describe the two parallel requests in the upload flow.", "a": "<p>(A) Add metadata: API servers save it with status \"pending\" and the notification service tells other clients a file is being uploaded. (B) Upload content: client → block servers → cloud storage; a completion callback to API servers sets status \"uploaded\", and the notification service tells other clients the file is fully uploaded.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Walk through the download flow.", "a": "<p>The client is notified (or catches up when it comes back online), fetches the change metadata from API servers (which read the metadata DB), then downloads the new blocks via block servers (which fetch them from cloud storage) and reconstructs the file.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Why long polling rather than WebSocket for notifications?", "a": "<p>Notifications only flow server → client, and they're infrequent with no bursts. WebSocket's bi-directional, real-time connection suits apps like chat. Dropbox uses long polling.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Name three ways to save storage space.", "a": "<p>De-duplicate identical blocks (same hash) at the account level. Use an intelligent backup strategy: cap the number of versions and keep only valuable ones, weighting recent versions. Move cold data to cheaper cold storage such as S3 Glacier.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "What happens when the metadata DB master fails, and when a notification server fails?", "a": "<p>DB master: promote a slave to master and add a new slave. Notification server: all its long-poll connections (over 1 million per machine, per Dropbox) drop, and clients must reconnect to other servers, which is relatively slow.</p>"}, {"ch": 15, "q": "Why not have clients upload directly to cloud storage?", "a": "<p>It's faster (one transfer instead of two), but chunking, compression and encryption would have to be built for iOS, Android and web, which is error-prone and costly. Clients can also be hacked, so client-side encryption isn't ideal. Block servers centralize that logic.</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "How should you split a 45-minute system design interview across the four steps?", "a": "<p>Understand the problem and scope: 3–10 min. High-level design and buy-in: 10–15 min. Deep dive: 10–25 min. Wrap up: 3–5 min. (<a href=\"chapters/03-interview-framework.html\">Ch 3</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "In master-slave replication, what happens when the master database goes offline?", "a": "<p>A slave is promoted to be the new master, and a new slave replaces the old one for replication. (<a href=\"chapters/01-scale-from-zero-to-millions.html\">Ch 1</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Which rate-limiting algorithm allows short bursts and is used by Amazon and Stripe?", "a": "<p>The <b>token bucket</b>. (<a href=\"chapters/04-rate-limiter.html\">Ch 4</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Why do virtual nodes improve consistent hashing?", "a": "<p>Each server owns many small partitions, so keys spread more evenly. The standard deviation is about 10% of the mean with 100 virtual nodes and 5% with 200. The cost is more space for the virtual-node metadata. (<a href=\"chapters/05-consistent-hashing.html\">Ch 5</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "With N replicas, write quorum W and read quorum R, when is strong consistency guaranteed?", "a": "<p>When <b>W + R &gt; N</b> (typically N = 3, W = R = 2), because at least one node overlaps between the write set and the read set. (<a href=\"chapters/06-key-value-store.html\">Ch 6</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "What is the Snowflake ID bit layout?", "a": "<p>1 sign | 41 timestamp | 5 datacenter | 5 machine | 12 sequence. (<a href=\"chapters/07-unique-id-generator.html\">Ch 7</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "URL shortener: 301 or 302 redirect if you care about click analytics?", "a": "<p><b>302</b> (temporary). Every click then reaches your servers. A 301 is cached by the browser, which reduces server load but hides repeat clicks. (<a href=\"chapters/08-url-shortener.html\">Ch 8</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Which data structures implement a crawler's \"URL Seen?\" check?", "a": "<p>A <b>Bloom filter</b> or a <b>hash table</b>. (<a href=\"chapters/09-web-crawler.html\">Ch 9</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "How does a notification system avoid losing notifications?", "a": "<p>It persists them in a <b>notification log</b> database and retries delivery. Dedupe by event ID because exactly-once delivery can't be guaranteed. (<a href=\"chapters/10-notification-system.html\">Ch 10</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "News feed: fanout on write or on read?", "a": "<p>A <b>hybrid</b>. Push (fanout on write) for most users, so reads are fast. Pull (fanout on read) for celebrities and users with huge follower counts, to avoid overloading the system. (<a href=\"chapters/11-news-feed.html\">Ch 11</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Why does the chat system use WebSocket?", "a": "<p>It's a persistent, <b>bi-directional</b> connection the server can push messages over. It starts as HTTP and is upgraded, and it uses ports 80/443, so it generally works through firewalls. (<a href=\"chapters/12-chat-system.html\">Ch 12</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Autocomplete: how do you make getting the top-k suggestions for a prefix fast?", "a": "<p>Limit the max prefix length and <b>cache the top-k queries at every trie node</b>. Both lookup steps then become ~O(1). (<a href=\"chapters/13-search-autocomplete.html\">Ch 13</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "YouTube: how do you let only authorized users upload straight to cloud storage?", "a": "<p>Give them a <b>pre-signed upload URL</b> from the API servers. (<a href=\"chapters/14-youtube.html\">Ch 14</a>)</p>"}, {"ch": 16, "q": "Google Drive: why split files into blocks?", "a": "<p>For <b>delta sync</b> (only modified blocks are uploaded) and per-block compression. Both save bandwidth. Dropbox caps blocks at 4 MB. (<a href=\"chapters/15-google-drive.html\">Ch 15</a>)</p>"}];
